│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ enhanced_ansatz_with_individual_params                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz uses parity-based parameter sharing (ry_even, ry_odd, rz_even, rz_odd, │
│                          │ cry_long_a, cry_long_b) which reduces parameters to 6 unique keys. However, this          │
│                          │ aggressive sharing may limit expressivity.                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The prior high-performing program (score 0.91) used more granular parameter sharing with  │
│                          │ 3 unique keys (ry_init, rz_main, ry_final, cry_nnn) but achieved better accuracy (0.93    │
│                          │ test vs 0.90 current).                                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This edit proposes a middle ground: use a hybrid approach with:                           │
│                          │ 1. Moderate parameter sharing on single-qubit rotations (2 keys instead of 4)             │
│                          │ 2. Simplified two-qubit entanglement with fewer but more focused parametrized gates       │
│                          │ 3. Removal of some fixed CZ gates to reduce depth while keeping parametrized CRY gates    │
│                          │ for flexibility                                                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This should improve test accuracy and generalization gap while keeping parameters         │
│                          │ reasonable.                                                                               │
