╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ decouple_cry_parameters                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The current program ties all 8 next-nearest-neighbor (NNN) CRY gates to a single          │
│                          │ parameter "cry_nnn", which limits the optimizer's ability to independently tune short-    │
│                          │ vs. long-range entanglement. The prior recommendation suggests introducing two separate   │
│                          │ parameter keys—one for nearest-neighbor (NN) CRY gates and one for NNN CRY gates—to allow │
│                          │ finer control.                                                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Analysis of prior runs:                                                                   │
│                          │ - The seed program achieved 0.91 combined score with N_PARAMS=26 (1 shared cry            │
│                          │ parameter).                                                                               │
│                          │ - The second program (with merged NN+NNN layers using same cry_nnn key) achieved 0.92     │
│                          │ combined score but with increased depth/gate count.                                       │
│                          │ - The current program is back to 0.91, indicating the merged approach provided marginal   │
│                          │ benefit.                                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The key insight: Instead of merging both NN and NNN into one layer with a shared          │
│                          │ parameter, we should:                                                                     │
│                          │ 1. Keep the structure simple (only NNN layer, no NN CRY added yet).                       │
│                          │ 2. Introduce TWO independent parameters: "cry_nn_param" for potential NN control and      │
│                          │ "cry_nnn_param" for true NNN, giving the optimizer independent tuning levers.             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This costs only +1 parameter per block (total 26 → 28 params), well within the efficiency │
│                          │ threshold where combined ≥0.92 is achievable. The decoupling allows the optimizer to      │
│                          │ learn different coupling strengths for near vs. next-nearest neighbors, potentially       │
│                          │ improving both convergence speed and final test accuracy.                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Implementation: Replace the single "cry_nnn" parameter with two distinct keys: "cry_nn"   │
│                          │ (for future NN CRY, currently unused) and "cry_nnn" (for existing NNN CRY gates). This    │
│                          │ provides the infrastructure to balance short- and long-range entanglement without         │
│                          │ increasing circuit depth.                                                                 │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
