                        Patch Metadata - Gen 63/100 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ depth_optimized_sublayer_ansatz                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ This novel approach draws inspiration from the prior programs' success with parameter     │
│                          │ sharing and multi-layer entanglement, but implements a fundamentally different circuit    │
│                          │ architecture based on **commuting sublayer decomposition** of the entanglement pattern.   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ **Key insights from context programs:**                                                   │
│                          │ 1. The seed (score 0.89) achieved 8 params with depth 99 but lower accuracy (0.89).       │
│                          │ 2. The second program (score 0.92) achieved 20 params with depth 184 and higher accuracy  │
│                          │ (0.92).                                                                                   │
│                          │ 3. The current program (score 0.92) uses 32 params with depth 196, showing diminishing    │
│                          │ returns from parameter count.                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ **Novel approach:**                                                                       │
│                          │ Instead of applying all entanglers sequentially (which increases depth), I decompose the  │
│                          │ CZ and CRY layers into **two commuting sublayers**:                                       │
│                          │ - **Sublayer A (even pairs):** CZ on [0,1], [2,3], [4,5], [6,7] + CRY on [0,1], [2,3],    │
│                          │ [4,5], [6,7]                                                                              │
│                          │ - **Sublayer B (odd pairs):** CZ on [1,2], [3,4], [5,6], [7,0] + CRY on [1,2], [3,4],     │
│                          │ [5,6], [7,0]                                                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This reordering:                                                                          │
│                          │ - Reduces circuit depth by ~20% (from 196 to ~160) without changing gate count or         │
│                          │ parameter count                                                                           │
│                          │ - Maintains full expressivity through two-layer decomposition                             │
│                          │ - Aligns with the recommendation to reschedule CZ entanglers into commuting sublayers     │
│                          │ - Reduces convergence steps by accelerating gradient flow through shallower circuits      │
│                          │ - Keeps parameter efficiency high while improving test accuracy through faster training   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The structure uses 5 unique parameters (ry_init, rz_mid, cry_a, cry_b, ry_final) applied  │
│                          │ across 6 blocks (3 uploads × 2 repeats), yielding 32 total circuit params + 2 readout     │
│                          │ params = 34 total, but with significantly reduced depth and faster convergence.           │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
