│ patch_description        │ The current program has a combined score of 0.92 with good validation accuracy (0.94) but │
│                          │ higher test loss (0.35) and a larger generalization gap (0.03) compared to prior          │
│                          │ programs. The recommendation suggests swapping the order of next-nearest-neighbor CRY     │
│                          │ before nearest-neighbor CRY to test how entanglement propagation ordering affects         │
│                          │ trainability.                                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Currently, the ansatz applies:                                                            │
│                          │ 1. Nearest-neighbor CRY (cry_nnn parameter, Layer 3)                                      │
│                          │ 2. Next-nearest-neighbor CRY (cry_nnn parameter, Layer 4)                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ By reversing this order—applying next-nearest-neighbor entanglement first, then           │
│                          │ nearest-neighbor—we can explore a different gradient landscape. This reordering may:      │
│                          │ - Improve generalization by establishing long-range correlations before local refinement  │
│                          │ - Reduce the train-test gap by providing better feature propagation                       │
│                          │ - Maintain the same parameter count and gate structure (zero-cost reordering)             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The key insight is that starting with longer-range entanglement may create a more         │
│                          │ favorable initialization landscape for the optimizer, potentially leading to better       │
│                          │ convergence and reduced overfitting.                                                      │
