                        Patch Metadata - Gen 73/100 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ expand_entanglement_with_shared_params                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ The current program achieves 0.89 combined score with only 5 parameters per block         │
│                          │ (ry_basis, cry_bottleneck, rz_readout, rx_main). The prior programs achieved 0.92 with 5  │
│                          │ parameters (first seed) and 0.92 with 6 parameters (second seed), suggesting we need      │
│                          │ richer entanglement structure.                                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The key insight from the prior programs is that they use:                                 │
│                          │ 1. Multiple parametrized two-qubit gates (CRY on nearest-neighbor AND                     │
│                          │ next-nearest-neighbor pairs)                                                              │
│                          │ 2. Shared parameters across these gates to keep parameter count low while enabling richer │
│                          │ expressivity                                                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The current program has a severe bottleneck: only ONE parametrized two-qubit gate         │
│                          │ (cry_bottleneck on [0,4]). This limits the circuit's ability to create complex            │
│                          │ entanglement patterns needed for classification.                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The recommendation suggests consolidating boundary RY layers into one shared key.         │
│                          │ However, the current program already does this efficiently (ry_basis is shared across all │
│                          │ 8 qubits). Instead, I'll follow the proven pattern from the second prior program:         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. Keep the efficient single-qubit layers (ry_basis, rz_readout, rx_main)                 │
│                          │ 2. Add parametrized nearest-neighbor CRY ring (8 gates, all sharing cry_nn parameter)     │
│                          │ 3. Add parametrized next-nearest-neighbor CRY (8 gates, all sharing cry_nnn parameter)    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This increases N_PARAMS_PER_BLOCK from 5 to 7 (adding cry_nn and cry_nnn), which is still │
│                          │ well below the 32-parameter limit and matches the structure that achieved 0.92 in prior   │
│                          │ runs. The shared parameters ensure efficient learning while the expanded connectivity     │
│                          │ enables richer feature extraction.                                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Expected improvements:                                                                    │
│                          │ - Better test accuracy (0.90 → 0.93+) through richer entanglement                         │
│                          │ - Reduced generalization gap through better feature learning                              │
│                          │ - Faster convergence through more expressive ansatz                                       │
│                          │ - Still maintains parameter efficiency                                                    │
